# Customize Appearance

## Introduction

You can always assemble a cropper of your own from the building blocks this library exports (see [Components / Hooks](/docs/guides/components-and-hooks)). The bundled `Cropper` is a thin layer over them, and most of its source is type declarations.

In practice you rarely need to go that far. The prebuilt cropper exposes a class name, a component prop or both for almost every layer it renders, so you can restyle or swap a single part and keep the rest. This guide walks through those layers from the outside in.

<Admonition type="tip" title="Styling another component's elements">

Class names you pass to the cropper end up on elements rendered by _other_ Svelte components, so a scoped selector in your component will never match them. Wrap such selectors in `:global(...)`, or put them in a global stylesheet.

</Admonition>

## Cropper Appearance

Leave the stencil aside for a moment. What remains is a wrapper element and, inside it, the image being cropped:

<CropperStructure />

### Wrapper

The wrapper is the outermost element of the cropper. Pass the `class` prop to add your own class to it, for example to change the colour behind the image or to give the cropper a height:

```svelte
<Cropper {src} class="my-cropper" />

<style>
	:global(.my-cropper) {
		height: 400px;
		background: #2b2b2b;
	}
</style>
```

To replace the wrapper altogether, pass a component to `wrapperComponent` (and its extra props through `wrapperProps`). A custom wrapper receives the `cropper` instance and a `children` snippet with the rest of the cropper, so it can surround that content with a loading indicator, toolbar buttons or navigation controls. The [Absolute Zoom tutorial](/docs/tutorials/absolute-zoom) builds one, and so does the Telegram demo in the [Showcase](/docs/showcase).

### Background Image

The background is the image you are cropping. Add a class to it with `backgroundClassName`, or replace the component with `backgroundComponent` (plus `backgroundProps`). A replacement is handy when the image needs extra treatment, such as the CSS filters in the [Image Editor tutorial](/docs/tutorials/image-editor).

<Admonition type="warning" title="Custom backgrounds need a bindable ref">

The cropper reads the rendered element to draw the result on a canvas, so a custom `backgroundComponent` must declare `let { ref = $bindable(null), ... } = $props()` and bind it to its `<img>` or `<canvas>` with `bind:this={ref}`.

</Admonition>

## Stencil Appearance

The most flexible option is writing a stencil of your own, covered step by step in the [Custom Stencil tutorial](/docs/tutorials/custom-stencil). For many designs, though, it is enough to adjust the stencils that ship with the library, [RectangleStencil](/docs/components/RectangleStencil) and [CircleStencil](/docs/components/CircleStencil). Both are built from the same parts, laid out over the background:

<StencilStructure />

### Basics

The cropper doesn't render the stencil props itself, it forwards whatever you put in `stencilProps` to the stencil component. Every option on this page goes there (see also [Pass props to the stencil](/docs/guides/recipes#pass-props-to-the-stencil)):

```svelte
<Cropper
	{src}
	stencilProps={{
		grid: true,
		previewClassName: 'my-preview'
	}}
/>
```

### Handler

Handlers are the draggable points used to resize the stencil. You can:

- replace the handler component,
- add your own class names to handlers,
- choose which of the eight positions show a handler.

#### Change handler component

Pass any Svelte component to `handlerComponent`. It is rendered once per enabled position and receives the props described by the exported `HandlerComponentProps` type (position, class names, `disabled`, and the `onMove` / `onMoveEnd` callbacks). The default is [SimpleHandler](/docs/components/SimpleHandler).

```svelte
<script lang="ts">
	import { Cropper } from 'svelte-advanced-cropper';
	import CustomHandler from './CustomHandler.svelte';
</script>

<Cropper
	{src}
	stencilProps={{
		handlerComponent: CustomHandler
	}}
/>
```

#### Customize the classnames

`handlerClassNames` takes an object. `default` is applied to every handler, `hover` while the pointer is over one, and each direction key targets a single handler:

```svelte
<Cropper
	{src}
	stencilProps={{
		handlerClassNames: {
			default: 'handler',
			hover: 'handler--hover',
			eastNorth: 'handler--east-north',
			north: 'handler--north',
			westNorth: 'handler--west-north',
			west: 'handler--west',
			westSouth: 'handler--west-south',
			south: 'handler--south',
			eastSouth: 'handler--east-south',
			east: 'handler--east'
		}
	}}
/>
```

Even if all you want is one class on every handler, it still goes inside the object:

```svelte
<Cropper
	{src}
	stencilProps={{
		handlerClassNames: { default: 'handler' }
	}}
/>
```

`handlerWrapperClassNames` accepts `default` and the direction keys, and targets the invisible, larger hit area around each handler.

#### Customize the list of handlers

`handlers` maps each of the eight positions to a boolean:

```svelte
<Cropper
	{src}
	stencilProps={{
		handlers: {
			eastNorth: true,
			north: false,
			westNorth: true,
			west: false,
			westSouth: true,
			south: false,
			eastSouth: true,
			east: false
		}
	}}
/>
```

<Admonition type="warning" title="Remember!">

Only positions set to `true` get a handler. A key you leave out counts as `false`, so passing an empty object hides every handler.

</Admonition>

### Lines

Lines are the four edges of the stencil, and dragging one resizes the stencil along a single axis. They are customized the same three ways:

- replace the component with `lineComponent` (default: [SimpleLine](/docs/components/SimpleLine), props typed as `LineComponentProps`),
- add class names with `lineClassNames` (`default`, `hover`, `disabled`, `north`, `east`, `south`, `west`), and `lineWrapperClassNames` for the hit areas,
- show or hide each edge with `lines`.

A `SimpleLine` is a zero-width box with a border on one side, so its look is controlled by border properties:

```css
.line {
	border-style: dashed;
	border-color: red;
}
```

<Admonition type="warning" title="Known issue">

Changing the line width with the `border-width` shorthand doesn't work well: it adds borders to every side of the thin line box, so the line ends up thicker than intended. Change only the side that is drawn for each position:

```css
.advanced-cropper-simple-line--west {
	border-left-width: 2px;
}
.advanced-cropper-simple-line--north {
	border-top-width: 2px;
}
.advanced-cropper-simple-line--east {
	border-right-width: 2px;
}
.advanced-cropper-simple-line--south {
	border-bottom-width: 2px;
}
```

</Admonition>

### Overlay

The overlay is the dimmed area around the stencil, and `overlayClassName` adds a class to it. It is drawn as a very large `box-shadow` coloured with `currentColor`, so there is not much to restyle beyond its colour, which you set through the `color` property:

```svelte
<Cropper
	{src}
	stencilProps={{
		overlayClassName: 'overlay'
	}}
/>

<style>
	:global(.overlay) {
		color: #601f55eb;
	}
</style>
```

<Example source={overlayColorSource} title="OverlayColorExample">
	<OverlayColorExample />
</Example>

### Preview

The preview is the element that marks the cropped area inside the stencil. You will seldom need to touch it, but `previewClassName` lets you, for example, outline it:

```svelte
<Cropper
	{src}
	stencilProps={{
		previewClassName: 'preview'
	}}
/>

<style>
	:global(.preview) {
		border: dashed 2px rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.45);
	}
</style>
```

<Example source={customPreviewSource} title="CustomPreviewExample">
	<CustomPreviewExample />
</Example>
